Make the event recorder's game-hash gate optional on playback.

Upstream, a recording stores the MD5 of the game's own files and a mismatch is FATAL:

    if (((_recordMode == kRecorderPlayback) || (_recordMode == kRecorderUpdate)) && !checkGameHash(desc)) {
        deinit();
        error("playback:action=error reason=\"\"");
    }

That is exactly right for what the recorder is FOR -- proving an ENGINE change did not regress a
KNOWN game, where a different game is a different experiment. We want the mirror image: the
engine is fixed and the GAME is what changes.

⭐ AS IT TURNS OUT WE DO NOT NEED THIS, and the measurement is worth recording. Our patches are
installed as LOOSE `*.SCR` patch files alongside untouched `RESOURCE.000/.001/.MAP`, and the
detection MD5s cover the volumes. So the hash is IDENTICAL across patch builds. Verified by
replaying one recording against two game dirs whose 0/12/18/203/204/206.SCR all differ: the gate
did not fire and playback proceeded.

So this patch is insurance for a build that ever rewrites the volumes, not a prerequisite. It is
opt-in and defaults to upstream behaviour.

    scummvm --record-mode=fast_playback --record-file-name=X --record_ignore_game_hash=true ...

⚠️ It does NOT make a replay meaningful across a script change -- the events replay identically
and the game's responses may not. Treat a warned replay as evidence only when the interaction
sites are unchanged.

⚠️ THE STACK ORDER MATTERS. This patch used to carry a COPY of 0002's ImGui build fix, which
meant 0002 and 0003 could not both be applied -- `git apply` failed on the second one, and
build_recorder_scummvm.sh papered over it by skipping 0002 whenever EventRecorder.cpp was already
modified. The duplicate hunks are gone; apply 0002 first and this on top.

FOR LOCAL DEBUGGING ONLY.
APPLY WITH: (cd tools/scummvm-ref && git apply ../scummvm-patches/0002-*.patch \
                                  && git apply ../scummvm-patches/0003-*.patch)

diff --git a/base/commandLine.cpp b/base/commandLine.cpp
index c29d1eb0..af239f8c 100644
--- a/base/commandLine.cpp
+++ b/base/commandLine.cpp
@@ -392,6 +392,9 @@ void registerDefaults() {
 	ConfMan.registerDefault("disable_display", false);
 #endif
 	ConfMan.registerDefault("record_mode", "none");
+	// LOCAL DEBUGGING PATCH -- see gui/EventRecorder.cpp. Lets a recording be replayed against a
+	// game whose files have changed, which upstream deliberately forbids.
+	ConfMan.registerDefault("record_ignore_game_hash", false);
 	ConfMan.registerDefault("record_file_name", "record.bin");
 
 	ConfMan.registerDefault("gui_saveload_chooser", "grid");
diff --git a/gui/EventRecorder.cpp b/gui/EventRecorder.cpp
index f8ec2dd3..9ffe7439 100644
--- a/gui/EventRecorder.cpp
+++ b/gui/EventRecorder.cpp
@@ -677,8 +677,24 @@ void EventRecorder::processGameDescription(const ADGameDescription *desc) {
 		setGameMd5(desc);
 	}
 	if (((_recordMode == kRecorderPlayback) || (_recordMode == kRecorderUpdate)) && !checkGameHash(desc)) {
-		deinit();
-		error("playback:action=error reason=\"\"");
+		// LOCAL DEBUGGING PATCH: warn instead of abort when the GAME FILES have changed.
+		//
+		// Upstream this check is exactly right: the recorder exists to prove that an ENGINE
+		// change did not regress a KNOWN game, so a different game is a different experiment.
+		// We want the mirror image -- the engine is fixed and the GAME is what changes, because
+		// every patch build rewrites the scripts. The hash gate makes every recording single-use
+		// for that purpose.
+		//
+		// Whether a replay stays MEANINGFUL across a script change is a separate question this
+		// does not answer: the events replay identically, and the game's responses may not.
+		// Treat a warned replay as evidence only when the interaction sites are unchanged.
+		if (ConfMan.getBool("record_ignore_game_hash")) {
+			warning("Event recorder: game files differ from the recording -- continuing anyway "
+			        "(record_ignore_game_hash=true). A desync here is expected, not a bug.");
+		} else {
+			deinit();
+			error("playback:action=error reason=\"\"");
+		}
 	}
 }
 
